+  <script type="application/ld+json">
+{
+  "@context": "https://schema.org",
+  "@type": "FAQPage",
+  "mainEntity": [
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "I'm a fresher — where do I start with DevOps?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "Start with Linux (not Windows). Spend 2–3 weeks getting comfortable with the terminal, file permissions, and Bash scripting. Then learn Git properly — not just git add/commit/push, but branching, merging, and rebasing. After that: Docker, then Kubernetes. Check my Resources page — I've listed everything I personally used in the exact order I'd recommend."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "Do I need a degree to get a DevOps job?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "No — but you need proof of skills. Recruiters want to see GitHub repos with real projects: a CI/CD pipeline, a Kubernetes deployment, an IaC setup on AWS. Build public projects, write a README explaining what problem each project solves, and put the GitHub link on your resume. That matters more than the degree in most DevOps hiring."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "What is AIOps and how does it relate to DevOps?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "AIOps (AI for IT Operations) uses machine learning and LLMs to automate what DevOps engineers currently do manually — anomaly detection, root cause analysis, and incident remediation. My research builds a multi-agent LLM system that watches a Kubernetes cluster via Prometheus + Loki + Jaeger and autonomously fixes issues. It's DevOps, but the on-call engineer is an AI. Check the AIOps page for details."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "Can we collaborate on a DevOps or AIOps project?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "Absolutely — I'm open to collaborations on open-source DevOps tooling, AIOps research, or cloud-native projects. Send me a message via the form above or reach out directly on LinkedIn with a brief description of what you're building. If it aligns with what I'm working on, I'll respond quickly."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "Which certifications do you recommend for beginners?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "Start with the Linux Foundation's free LFS101 (Introduction to Linux) — it's free and covers exactly what you need. Then AWS Cloud Practitioner for cloud fundamentals. Once you're comfortable with Kubernetes, go for CKA (Certified Kubernetes Administrator) — it's the most respected hands-on cert in DevOps. See my full list on the Resources page."
+      }
+    },
+    {
+      "@type": "Question",
+      "name": "I'm a recruiter — is Saad open to opportunities?",
+      "acceptedAnswer": {
+        "@type": "Answer",
+        "text": "Yes — I'm open to DevOps Engineer, Cloud Engineer, SRE, or AIOps-related roles. I have hands-on experience with Kubernetes, GitLab CI/CD (self-hosted), Terraform, Ansible, ArgoCD, Prometheus/Grafana/Loki, and AWS. I'm also an active researcher with a paper under IEEE TNSM review. Reach out via email or LinkedIn — I'll respond within 12 hours."
+      }
+    }
+  ]
+}
