+
+      <!-- ── 3 ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
+      <article class="pj-card" id="cicd-pipeline" data-level="beginner">
+        <div class="pj-head">
+          <div class="pj-num">03</div>
+          <div>
+            <h2>CI/CD Pipeline with GitHub Actions</h2>
+            <p>Every pull request runs lint, tests and a security scan. Every merge to main builds and pushes a versioned image to a registry.</p>
+            <div class="pj-meta">
+              <span class="pj-pill lvl-beginner">Beginner</span>
+              <span class="pj-pill">~1 week</span>
+              <span class="pj-pill">GitHub Actions</span><span class="pj-pill">GHCR</span><span class="pj-pill">Trivy</span>
+            </div>
+          </div>
+          <label class="pj-done"><input type="checkbox" data-project="cicd-pipeline"> Completed</label>
+        </div>
+        <div class="pj-body">
+          <details>
+            <summary>Architecture</summary>
+            <div class="pj-section">
+              <div class="pj-arch">PR → lint → unit tests → docker build → trivy scan ✔ → merge → build → push ghcr.io/you/app:sha &amp; :latest</div>
+              <p>Reuse the API from Project 02.</p>
+            </div>
+          </details>
+          <details>
+            <summary>Steps</summary>
+            <div class="pj-section"><ol>
+              <li><strong>Add tests</strong> to the API, even just 3–5, so the pipeline has something to run.</li>
+              <li><strong>Create <code>.github/workflows/ci.yml</code></strong> that runs on <code>pull_request</code> and on <code>push</code> to <code>main</code>.</li>
+              <li><strong>Cache dependencies</strong> with the setup action's built-in cache.</li>
+              <li><strong>Scan the image</strong> with Trivy and fail the job on CRITICAL vulnerabilities.</li>
+              <li><strong>Push to GHCR</strong> using the built-in <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>, so no personal tokens are needed. Tag images with the commit SHA. Image names must be lowercase, so use a lowercase repo name or set the image name yourself.</li>
+              <li><strong>Protect <code>main</code>:</strong> require the CI check to pass before merging.</li>
+            </ol>
+            <pre><code>name: ci
+on:
+  pull_request:
+  push:
+    branches: [main]
+
+permissions:
+  contents: read
+  packages: write
+
+jobs:
+  build:
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    steps:
+      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
+      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
+        with: { python-version: "3.12", cache: pip }
+      - run: pip install -r api/requirements.txt &amp;&amp; pytest api
+      - run: docker build -t ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}:${{ github.sha }} api
+      - uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@0.28.0
+        with:
+          image-ref: ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}:${{ github.sha }}
+          severity: CRITICAL
+          exit-code: "1"
+      - if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'
+        run: |
+          echo "${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}" | docker login ghcr.io -u ${{ github.actor }} --password-stdin
+          docker push ghcr.io/${{ github.repository }}:${{ github.sha }}</code></pre></div>
+          </details>
+          <details>
+            <summary>Done when</summary>
+            <div class="pj-section"><ul>
+              <li>A PR with a failing test cannot be merged</li>
+              <li>Merging to main produces a new image in your repo's <em>Packages</em> tab, tagged with the commit SHA</li>
+              <li>The README shows a passing CI status badge</li>
+            </ul></div>
+          </details>
+          <details>
+            <summary>Stretch goals &amp; résumé bullet</summary>
+            <div class="pj-section">
+              <ul>
+                <li>Add a matrix build across two language versions</li>
+                <li>Move the build logic into a reusable workflow and call it from a second repo</li>
+                <li>Generate an SBOM with Syft and attach it as a build artifact</li>
+              </ul>
+              <p style="margin-top:0.9rem;" class="pj-resume">"Built a GitHub Actions pipeline with automated tests, container vulnerability scanning and SHA-tagged image publishing, gating every merge to main."</p>
+            </div>
+          </details>
+        </div>
+      </article>
+
+      <!-- ── 4 ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
+      <article class="pj-card" id="terraform-aws" data-level="intermediate">
+        <div class="pj-head">
+          <div class="pj-num">04</div>
+          <div>
+            <h2>Terraform an AWS Network &amp; Web Tier</h2>
+            <p>Provision a VPC, public and private subnets, a load balancer and an auto-scaling group using reusable modules and remote state.</p>
+            <div class="pj-meta">
+              <span class="pj-pill lvl-intermediate">Intermediate</span>
+              <span class="pj-pill">~1–2 weeks</span>
+              <span class="pj-pill">Terraform</span><span class="pj-pill">AWS</span><span class="pj-pill">S3 state</span><span class="pj-pill">IAM</span>
+            </div>
+          </div>
+          <label class="pj-done"><input type="checkbox" data-project="terraform-aws"> Completed</label>
+        </div>
+        <div class="pj-body">
+          <details>
+            <summary>Architecture</summary>
+            <div class="pj-section">
+              <div class="pj-arch">VPC 10.0.0.0/16 → 2 public subnets (ALB) + 2 private subnets (ASG of EC2 running your container) → NAT → internet</div>
+              <p>⚠ An ALB and a NAT gateway are <strong>not</strong> free. Expect a few dollars per day, so destroy everything when you finish each session. For a free variant, skip the NAT and put the instances in public subnets with tight security groups.</p>
+            </div>
+          </details>
+          <details>
+            <summary>Steps</summary>
+            <div class="pj-section"><ol>
+              <li><strong>Bootstrap state.</strong> Create an S3 bucket (versioning on) for remote state, and enable S3 native locking with <code>use_lockfile = true</code> (Terraform 1.10+).</li>
+              <li><strong>Write a <code>network</code> module:</strong> VPC, subnets across 2 AZs, route tables, IGW and NAT.</li>
+              <li><strong>Write a <code>web</code> module:</strong> launch template whose user-data runs your Docker image, an ASG (min 2) and an ALB with a health-checked target group.</li>
+              <li><strong>Use environments.</strong> Create <code>envs/dev</code> and <code>envs/prod</code> folders that call the same modules with different variables.</li>
+              <li><strong>Add guardrails:</strong> <code>terraform fmt -check</code>, <code>terraform validate</code> and <code>tflint</code> in CI, with <code>terraform plan</code> output posted to PRs.</li>
+            </ol>
+            <pre><code>terraform {
+  required_version = "&gt;= 1.10"
+  backend "s3" {
+    bucket       = "yourname-tfstate"
+    key          = "dev/web/terraform.tfstate"
+    region       = "us-east-1"
+    use_lockfile = true
+  }
+}
+
+module "network" {
+  source   = "../../modules/network"
+  cidr     = "10.0.0.0/16"
+  az_count = 2
+}
+
+module "web" {
+  source          = "../../modules/web"
+  vpc_id          = module.network.vpc_id
+  public_subnets  = module.network.public_subnet_ids
+  private_subnets = module.network.private_subnet_ids
+  image           = "ghcr.io/you/app:latest"
+  min_size        = 2
+}</code></pre></div>
+          </details>
+          <details>
+            <summary>Done when</summary>
+            <div class="pj-section"><ul>
+              <li>The ALB DNS name serves your app, and terminating an instance gets it replaced automatically</li>
+              <li>A second <code>terraform plan</code> reports <em>No changes</em></li>
+              <li><code>terraform destroy</code> removes everything cleanly</li>
+            </ul></div>
+          </details>
+          <details>
+            <summary>Stretch goals &amp; résumé bullet</summary>
+            <div class="pj-section">
+              <ul>
+                <li>Use GitHub OIDC to assume an AWS role from CI, with no long-lived access keys</li>
+                <li>Add HTTPS with ACM and a Route 53 record</li>
+                <li>Add <code>checkov</code> or <code>trivy config</code> to scan the Terraform code</li>
+              </ul>
+              <p style="margin-top:0.9rem;" class="pj-resume">"Designed reusable Terraform modules for a multi-AZ AWS VPC and auto-scaling web tier behind an ALB, with S3 remote state and plan-on-PR CI."</p>
+            </div>
+          </details>
+        </div>
+      </article>
+
+      <!-- ── 5 ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
+      <article class="pj-card" id="k8s-helm" data-level="intermediate">
+        <div class="pj-head">
+          <div class="pj-num">05</div>
+          <div>
+            <h2>Deploy to Kubernetes with Helm</h2>
+            <p>Run the 3-tier app on a local Kubernetes cluster with a Helm chart, Ingress, probes, resource limits and autoscaling.</p>
