-  'k8s-beginners': {
-    title: 'Kubernetes for Beginners: Pods, Deployments & Services Explained',
-    meta: '⚙️ Kubernetes · Feb 20, 2026 · 12 min read · Beginner',
-    body: `
-      <h1>Kubernetes for Beginners: Pods, Deployments & Services Explained</h1>
-      <div class="blog-meta" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
-        <span class="blog-meta-item">⚙️ Kubernetes</span>
-        <span class="blog-meta-item">📅 Feb 20, 2026</span>
-        <span class="blog-meta-item">⏱ 12 min read</span>
-      </div>
-
-      <p>Kubernetes (K8s) is the de facto standard for running containerised applications at scale. It automates deployment, scaling, and management of containers across a cluster of machines. Here's what every beginner needs to know.</p>
-
-      <h2>Core Objects</h2>
-
-      <h3>Pod — The Smallest Unit</h3>
-      <p>A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. It wraps one or more containers that share network and storage.</p>
-      <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
-kind: Pod
-metadata:
-  name: my-app
-spec:
-  containers:
-  - name: app
-    image: nginx:alpine
-    ports:
-    - containerPort: 80</code></pre>
-
-      <h3>Deployment — Managing Replicas</h3>
-      <p>You almost never create Pods directly. Use a Deployment to manage multiple replicas with rolling updates and rollbacks.</p>
-      <pre><code>apiVersion: apps/v1
-kind: Deployment
-metadata:
-  name: my-app
-spec:
-  replicas: 3
-  selector:
-    matchLabels:
-      app: my-app
-  template:
-    metadata:
-      labels:
-        app: my-app
-    spec:
-      containers:
-      - name: app
-        image: myapp:1.0
-        resources:
-          requests:
-            memory: "64Mi"
-            cpu: "250m"
-          limits:
-            memory: "128Mi"
-            cpu: "500m"</code></pre>
-
-      <h3>Service — Networking</h3>
-      <p>A Service exposes your Pods via a stable DNS name and IP address, load balancing across all healthy Pods.</p>
-      <pre><code>apiVersion: v1
-kind: Service
-metadata:
-  name: my-app-service
-spec:
-  selector:
-    app: my-app
-  ports:
-  - port: 80
-    targetPort: 8080
-  type: ClusterIP</code></pre>
-
-      <h2>Essential kubectl Commands</h2>
-      <pre><code># Apply manifest
-kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
-
-# Get resources
-kubectl get pods
-kubectl get deployments
-kubectl get services
-
-# Describe a resource
-kubectl describe pod my-app-abc123
-
-# View logs
-kubectl logs my-app-abc123 -f
-
-# Execute into a container
-kubectl exec -it my-app-abc123 -- /bin/sh
-
-# Scale a deployment
-kubectl scale deployment my-app --replicas=5
-
-# Rolling update
-kubectl set image deployment/my-app app=myapp:2.0
-
-# Rollback
-kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-app</code></pre>
-
-      <div class="article-callout">
-        <p><strong>Practice tip:</strong> Use <code>kind</code> or <code>minikube</code> to run a local Kubernetes cluster on your laptop for free. No cloud account needed.</p>
-      </div>
-    `
-  },
-
-  'terraform-aws': {
-    title: 'Terraform on AWS: Provision Your First Cloud Infrastructure',
-    meta: '🏗️ Terraform · Mar 5, 2026 · 15 min read · Intermediate',
-    body: `
-      <h1>Terraform on AWS: Provision Your First Cloud Infrastructure</h1>
-      <div class="blog-meta" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
-        <span class="blog-meta-item">🏗️ Terraform</span>
-        <span class="blog-meta-item">📅 Mar 5, 2026</span>
-        <span class="blog-meta-item">⏱ 15 min read</span>
-      </div>
-
-      <p>Terraform lets you define your entire cloud infrastructure as code. Instead of clicking through the AWS console and forgetting what you did, everything is versioned, reviewable, and repeatable.</p>
-
-      <h2>Installation & Setup</h2>
-      <pre><code># Install Terraform (macOS)
-brew install terraform
-
-# Verify
-terraform version
-
-# Configure AWS credentials
-aws configure</code></pre>
-
-      <h2>Project Structure</h2>
-      <pre><code>my-infra/
-├── main.tf          # Core resources
-├── variables.tf     # Input variables
-├── outputs.tf       # Output values
-├── terraform.tfvars # Variable values
-└── versions.tf      # Provider versions</code></pre>
-
-      <h2>Provision a VPC + EC2 Instance</h2>
-      <pre><code># versions.tf
-terraform {
-  required_providers {
-    aws = {
-      source  = "hashicorp/aws"
-      version = "~> 5.0"
-    }
-  }
-}
-
-provider "aws" {
-  region = var.region
-}
-
-# main.tf
-resource "aws_vpc" "main" {
-  cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16"
-  tags = { Name = "devops-hub-vpc" }
-}
-
-resource "aws_subnet" "public" {
-  vpc_id     = aws_vpc.main.id
-  cidr_block = "10.0.1.0/24"
-  map_public_ip_on_launch = true
-}
-
-resource "aws_instance" "web" {
-  ami           = "ami-0c02fb55956c7d316"
-  instance_type = "t3.micro"
-  subnet_id     = aws_subnet.public.id
-  tags = { Name = "web-server" }
-}</code></pre>
-
-      <h2>The Terraform Workflow</h2>
-      <pre><code># Initialise — download providers
-terraform init
-
-# Preview changes
-terraform plan
-
-# Apply changes
-terraform apply
-
-# Destroy resources
-terraform destroy</code></pre>
-
-      <div class="article-callout">
-        <p><strong>Always review</strong> <code>terraform plan</code> output before applying. It shows exactly what will be created, modified, or destroyed.</p>
-      </div>
-    `
-  },
