-  'devsecops-pipeline': {
-    title: 'How to Add Security Scanning to Your CI/CD Pipeline',
-    meta: '🛡️ DevSecOps · Apr 2, 2026 · 9 min read · Advanced',
-    body: `
-      <h1>How to Add Security Scanning to Your CI/CD Pipeline</h1>
-      <div class="blog-meta" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
-        <span class="blog-meta-item">🛡️ Security</span>
-        <span class="blog-meta-item">📅 Apr 2, 2026</span>
-        <span class="blog-meta-item">⏱ 9 min read</span>
-      </div>
-
-      <p>Security shouldn't be an afterthought reviewed at the end of a release cycle. Shifting left means integrating security checks into every stage of your CI/CD pipeline — automatically, on every commit.</p>
-
-      <h2>Four Layers of Pipeline Security</h2>
-      <ul>
-        <li><strong>SAST</strong> — Static Application Security Testing (source code analysis)</li>
-        <li><strong>Dependency scanning</strong> — Check third-party libraries for known CVEs</li>
-        <li><strong>Container scanning</strong> — Scan Docker images for vulnerabilities</li>
-        <li><strong>Secrets detection</strong> — Find accidentally committed credentials</li>
-      </ul>
-
-      <h2>Complete Security Pipeline</h2>
-      <pre><code>name: Security Pipeline
-
-on: [push, pull_request]
-
-jobs:
-  sast:
-    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
-    steps:
-      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
-      - name: Run Semgrep SAST
-        uses: semgrep/semgrep-action@v1
-        with:
-          config: p/owasp-top-ten
-
-  dependency-scan:
-    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
-    steps:
-      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
-      - name: Dependency audit
-        run: npm audit --audit-level=high
-
-  container-scan:
-    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
-    steps:
-      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
-      - name: Build image
-        run: docker build -t myapp:test .
-      - name: Trivy container scan
-        uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@master
-        with:
-          image-ref: myapp:test
-          severity: HIGH,CRITICAL
-          exit-code: 1
-
-  secrets-scan:
-    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
-    steps:
-      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
-        with:
-          fetch-depth: 0
-      - name: GitLeaks secrets scan
-        uses: gitleaks/gitleaks-action@v2</code></pre>
-
-      <div class="article-callout">
-        <p><strong>Golden rule:</strong> Set <code>exit-code: 1</code> on CRITICAL findings so the pipeline fails and blocks the merge. Security gates only work if they're enforced.</p>
-      </div>
-    `
-  },
-
-  'dora-metrics': {
-    title: 'DORA Metrics Explained: Measure and Improve Your DevOps Performance',
-    meta: '📈 Culture · Apr 10, 2026 · 7 min read · All levels',
-    body: `
-      <h1>DORA Metrics Explained: Measure and Improve Your DevOps Performance</h1>
-      <div class="blog-meta" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
-        <span class="blog-meta-item">📈 Culture & SRE</span>
-        <span class="blog-meta-item">📅 Apr 10, 2026</span>
-        <span class="blog-meta-item">⏱ 7 min read</span>
-      </div>
-
-      <p>How do you know if your DevOps practices are actually improving? The DORA (DevOps Research and Assessment) team spent years analysing thousands of organisations and identified four metrics that reliably predict software delivery performance.</p>
-
-      <h2>The Four DORA Metrics</h2>
-
-      <h3>1. Deployment Frequency</h3>
-      <p>How often your team successfully deploys to production. Elite teams deploy multiple times per day. Low performers deploy monthly or less frequently.</p>
-
-      <h3>2. Lead Time for Changes</h3>
-      <p>The time from a code commit to that code running in production. Elite: less than one hour. Low performers: 6 months or more.</p>
-
-      <h3>3. Mean Time to Restore (MTTR)</h3>
-      <p>How long it takes to recover from a production failure. Elite teams restore in less than one hour using automated rollbacks and runbooks.</p>
-
-      <h3>4. Change Failure Rate</h3>
-      <p>The percentage of deployments that cause a production failure requiring a fix or rollback. Elite: 0–5%. High performers maintain this through automated testing and canary deployments.</p>
-
-      <h2>Elite vs Low Performer Benchmarks</h2>
-      <pre><code>Metric                  Elite         Low Performer
-──────────────────────────────────────────────────
-Deployment Frequency    Multiple/day  < Once/month
-Lead Time               < 1 hour      6+ months
-MTTR                    < 1 hour      1+ week
-Change Failure Rate     0–5%          46–60%</code></pre>
-
-      <h2>How to Start Measuring</h2>
-      <ul>
-        <li>Track deployment timestamps in your CI/CD system</li>
-        <li>Record incident start and resolution times</li>
-        <li>Tag production incidents caused by deployments</li>
-        <li>Calculate lead time from first commit to deploy timestamp</li>
-      </ul>
-
-      <div class="article-callout">
-        <p><strong>Key finding:</strong> DORA research shows these metrics are leading indicators of organisational performance — teams that score Elite are twice as likely to exceed profitability and productivity goals.</p>
-      </div>
-    `
-  }
-};
