+<p>AI coding assistants and LLM agents are now part of everyday engineering work. In DevOps they are genuinely useful for some jobs and dangerous for others. This guide covers where they help today, where they don't, and the guardrails that let you use them safely on real infrastructure.</p>
+
+<h2>Where AI helps today</h2>
+<table>
+  <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>How AI helps</th><th>Risk level</th></tr></thead>
+  <tbody>
+    <tr><td>Writing IaC, YAML and scripts</td><td>Drafts Terraform modules, Helm values, Dockerfiles and Bash in seconds</td><td>Medium: must be reviewed and tested</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Explaining unfamiliar code and config</td><td>"What does this Helm chart do?" and "Why is this regex failing?"</td><td>Low</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Reading logs and errors</td><td>Summarises a 500-line stack trace and suggests likely causes</td><td>Low (if the logs contain no secrets)</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Code review</td><td>Automated PR review catches bugs, missing tests and risky changes</td><td>Low: advisory only</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>CI failure triage</td><td>Reads failing job logs and proposes a fix or opens a PR</td><td>Medium: the fix still goes through review and CI</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Incident investigation (AIOps)</td><td>Correlates metrics, logs, traces and recent deploys to suggest a root cause</td><td>Medium: humans decide what to do</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Autonomous remediation</td><td>An agent restarts, scales or rolls back on its own</td><td>High: needs strict guardrails</td></tr>
+  </tbody>
+</table>
+
+<h2>Generating infrastructure code safely</h2>
+<p>An LLM can produce a convincing Terraform module that opens <code>0.0.0.0/0</code> on port 22, or uses a deprecated provider argument. Treat AI output like a pull request from a fast but junior colleague:</p>
+<ol>
+  <li><strong>Give context</strong>: provider versions, naming conventions, existing modules, and your constraints (e.g. "private subnets only, encrypted at rest, tags required").</li>
+  <li><strong>Run the same gates as human code</strong>: <code>terraform fmt</code>/<code>validate</code>, <code>tflint</code>, <code>checkov</code> or <code>trivy config</code>, <code>kubeconform</code>, <code>shellcheck</code>.</li>
+  <li><strong>Read the plan</strong>: <code>terraform plan</code> is the ground truth, whatever the prose explanation says.</li>
+  <li><strong>Test in a sandbox account or cluster</strong> before anything reaches production.</li>
+</ol>
+<pre><code># A practical review loop for generated IaC
+terraform fmt -check -recursive
+terraform validate
+tflint --recursive
+trivy config .            # misconfiguration scan (open SGs, unencrypted storage…)
+terraform plan -out=tf.plan &amp;&amp; terraform show -json tf.plan | jq '.resource_changes[] | {address, actions: .change.actions}'</code></pre>
+
+<h2>AI in the CI/CD pipeline</h2>
+<p>Common patterns teams adopt:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>AI PR review</strong> as a non-blocking check that comments on risky diffs, missing tests or secrets.</li>
+  <li><strong>Failure summaries</strong>: when a job fails, an LLM posts a short "what broke and the likely fix" comment, so nobody has to scroll through logs.</li>
+  <li><strong>Agentic fixes</strong>: a coding agent proposes a commit for a failing test or a dependency bump. It still goes through the normal pipeline and human approval.</li>
+</ul>
+<div class="article-callout warn">
+  <p><strong>Prompt injection is real:</strong> PR descriptions, issue comments, logs and even dependency READMEs can contain text crafted to steer an AI agent. Give agents that read untrusted input <em>no</em> secrets and <em>no</em> write access to protected branches or production.</p>
+</div>
+
+<h2>Agents, tools and MCP</h2>
+<p>An <strong>agent</strong> is an LLM in a loop that can call tools: run <code>kubectl get</code>, query Prometheus, read a runbook, open a ticket. The <strong>Model Context Protocol (MCP)</strong> is an open standard for exposing such tools and data sources to AI applications in a consistent way. Many platforms (Kubernetes, cloud providers, observability tools, Git hosts) now ship MCP servers.</p>
+<p>Design tool access the same way you design IAM:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Read-only by default.</strong> Investigation tools (<code>get</code>, <code>describe</code>, <code>logs</code>, PromQL queries) cover most of the value.</li>
+  <li><strong>Narrow write tools</strong> such as "restart deployment X in namespace Y" or "scale within min/max", not a raw shell or <code>kubectl apply</code>.</li>
+  <li><strong>Human approval</strong> for anything that changes production, with the agent's reasoning and proposed command shown.</li>
+  <li><strong>Audit everything</strong>: every tool call is logged with who or what triggered it.</li>
+  <li><strong>Separate credentials</strong>: the agent's identity has its own tightly scoped role, never a human's admin token.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2>AIOps: AI for incident response</h2>
+<p>AIOps applies ML and LLMs to operations data. A typical multi-agent setup:</p>
+<pre><code>Alert fires (Prometheus / Alertmanager)
+   │
+   ▼
+Triage agent ── reads: alert, recent deploys, SLO burn rate
+   │
+   ├─► Metrics agent  ── PromQL: error rate, latency, saturation by pod/node
+   ├─► Logs agent     ── Loki: error patterns around the start time
+   └─► Traces agent   ── Jaeger/Tempo: slowest spans, failing dependency
+   │
+   ▼
+Root-cause hypothesis + evidence + suggested runbook action
+   │
+   ▼
+Human approves → guarded action (rollback / scale / restart) → verify SLIs recover</code></pre>
+<p>The value isn't magic root-cause detection. It's <strong>compressing the first 15 minutes of an incident</strong>, the gathering of context, into seconds, while humans keep the decisions. Read more about this architecture on the <a href="aiops.html">AIOps page</a>.</p>
+
+<h2>MLOps in one section</h2>
+<p>If your team ships ML models, the DevOps principles carry over with a few additions:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Version data and models</strong>, not just code (DVC, MLflow model registry, artifacts in object storage).</li>
+  <li><strong>Pipelines for training</strong> (Kubeflow, Argo Workflows, managed services) as well as for deployment.</li>
+  <li><strong>Serving</strong> with autoscaling GPUs and specialised inference servers (vLLM, KServe, Triton).</li>
+  <li><strong>Monitoring</strong> for model quality and data drift, not only latency and errors.</li>
+  <li><strong>Cost control</strong>: GPUs are expensive, so track utilisation, batch requests, and right-size the model.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2>A sensible adoption path</h2>
+<ol>
+  <li>Use an AI assistant in your editor and terminal for drafts and explanations, and review everything.</li>
+  <li>Add non-blocking AI PR review and CI failure summaries.</li>
+  <li>Give a read-only investigation agent access to metrics, logs and runbooks.</li>
+  <li>Only then automate narrow, reversible actions, behind approval and with full audit logs.</li>
+</ol>
+
+<div class="article-callout">
+  <p><strong>Skills still matter:</strong> AI makes strong engineers faster, but you can only judge its output if you understand Linux, networking, Kubernetes and IaC yourself. Use the <a href="roadmap.html">roadmap</a> and <a href="projects.html">projects</a> to build that foundation.</p>
+</div>
diff --git a/_blog/posts/aws-core-services.html b/_blog/posts/aws-core-services.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..aea3349
--- /dev/null
+++ b/_blog/posts/aws-core-services.html
@@ -0,0 +1,114 @@
+<p>AWS has over 200 services, but a DevOps engineer uses about fifteen of them daily. This guide maps those core services to the jobs they do, shows how they fit together in a standard architecture, and covers the security and cost habits that keep you out of trouble.</p>
+
+<div class="article-callout warn">
+  <p><strong>Before you touch anything:</strong> turn on MFA for the root user and then stop using root. Create an AWS Budget with an email alert (say, $10/month), and use an IAM Identity Center user or role for daily work.</p>
+</div>
+
+<h2>Global infrastructure</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Region</strong> (e.g. <code>eu-west-1</code>): a geographic area. Data stays in the region unless you move it.</li>
+  <li><strong>Availability Zone</strong> (e.g. <code>eu-west-1a</code>): one or more isolated data centres within a region. Spread across at least two AZs for high availability.</li>
+  <li><strong>Edge locations:</strong> CloudFront and Route 53 points of presence close to users.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2>The core services map</h2>
+<table>
+  <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Service</th><th>One-line summary</th></tr></thead>
+  <tbody>
+    <tr><td>Identity</td><td><strong>IAM</strong>, IAM Identity Center</td><td>Who can do what, on which resources</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Networking</td><td><strong>VPC</strong></td><td>Your private network: subnets, routes, gateways, security groups</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Virtual machines</td><td><strong>EC2</strong> + Auto Scaling</td><td>Instances, and groups that heal and scale themselves</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Load balancing</td><td><strong>ELB</strong> (ALB / NLB)</td><td>L7 HTTP routing or L4 TCP/UDP</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Containers</td><td><strong>ECR</strong>, <strong>ECS</strong>, <strong>EKS</strong>, Fargate</td><td>Registry, AWS's own orchestrator, managed Kubernetes, serverless compute for both</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Serverless</td><td><strong>Lambda</strong>, API Gateway</td><td>Run functions on events; no servers to manage</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Object storage</td><td><strong>S3</strong></td><td>Practically unlimited files: artifacts, backups, static sites, Terraform state</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Block storage</td><td><strong>EBS</strong></td><td>Disks for EC2 (and Kubernetes PVs)</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Databases</td><td><strong>RDS</strong>/Aurora, DynamoDB</td><td>Managed relational databases; serverless key-value</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>DNS and CDN</td><td><strong>Route 53</strong>, <strong>CloudFront</strong></td><td>Domains and health-checked routing; a global cache</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Observability</td><td><strong>CloudWatch</strong>, X-Ray</td><td>Metrics, logs, alarms, traces</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Audit</td><td><strong>CloudTrail</strong></td><td>Who called which API, and when. Turn it on everywhere.</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Secrets</td><td><strong>Secrets Manager</strong>, SSM Parameter Store, KMS</td><td>Store and rotate secrets; manage encryption keys</td></tr>
+  </tbody>
+</table>
+
+<h2>IAM: the most important service</h2>
+<p>Every AWS API call is authorised by IAM. The key concepts:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Principals:</strong> users (avoid long-lived keys), <strong>roles</strong> (assumed for temporary credentials) and AWS services.</li>
+  <li><strong>Policies:</strong> JSON documents that <code>Allow</code> or <code>Deny</code> actions on resources. An explicit deny always wins.</li>
+  <li><strong>Least privilege:</strong> grant only the actions and resources a workload needs.</li>
+</ul>
+<pre><code>{
+  "Version": "2012-10-17",
+  "Statement": [{
+    "Effect": "Allow",
+    "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject"],
+    "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::my-app-artifacts/*"
+  }]
+}</code></pre>
+<p>Workloads should never use access keys. EC2 gets an <strong>instance profile</strong>, EKS pods use <strong>EKS Pod Identity</strong> or IRSA, Lambda gets an execution role, and CI uses <strong>OIDC federation</strong> (e.g. GitHub Actions assuming a role).</p>
+
+<h2>A standard VPC design</h2>
+<pre><code>VPC 10.0.0.0/16
+├── AZ a
+│   ├── public  10.0.0.0/24   → ALB, NAT Gateway        (route 0.0.0.0/0 → Internet Gateway)
+│   └── private 10.0.10.0/24  → app instances / EKS nodes (route 0.0.0.0/0 → NAT Gateway)
+├── AZ b
+│   ├── public  10.0.1.0/24
+│   └── private 10.0.11.0/24
+└── data subnets (no internet route) → RDS, ElastiCache</code></pre>
+<ul>
+  <li>Only load balancers (and NAT) live in public subnets. Everything else is private.</li>
+  <li>Security groups can reference each other. For example, "the DB accepts 5432 only from the app's security group". That beats IP ranges.</li>
+  <li><strong>VPC endpoints</strong> for S3, ECR and others keep traffic private and cut NAT data-processing charges.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2>Compute: EC2, containers or Lambda?</h2>
+<table>
+  <thead><tr><th>Choose</th><th>When</th></tr></thead>
+  <tbody>
+    <tr><td>EC2 + Auto Scaling</td><td>Legacy or stateful software, special OS needs, full control</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>ECS on Fargate</td><td>Containers without managing servers or Kubernetes; the simplest container option on AWS</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>EKS</td><td>You want the Kubernetes ecosystem (Helm, Argo CD, operators) or portability</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Lambda</td><td>Event-driven or spiky workloads, glue code, scheduled jobs; short-running tasks (max 15 minutes)</td></tr>
+  </tbody>
+</table>
+
+<h2>The AWS CLI in 2 minutes</h2>
+<pre><code>aws configure sso                       # log in via IAM Identity Center
+aws sts get-caller-identity             # who am I right now?
+aws s3 ls s3://my-bucket/ --recursive --human-readable
+aws s3 sync ./dist s3://my-site-bucket --delete
+aws ec2 describe-instances \
+  --filters "Name=tag:env,Values=prod" \
+  --query "Reservations[].Instances[].[InstanceId,State.Name,PrivateIpAddress]" \
+  --output table
+aws logs tail /aws/lambda/my-fn --follow
+aws ecr get-login-password | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin &lt;acct&gt;.dkr.ecr.&lt;region&gt;.amazonaws.com
+aws eks update-kubeconfig --name my-cluster --region eu-west-1</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Cost habits that matter</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Tag everything</strong> (<code>env</code>, <code>team</code>, <code>service</code>) and enable cost allocation tags, so Cost Explorer can tell you who spends what.</li>
+  <li>Watch the usual surprises: <strong>NAT Gateway</strong> hourly and per-GB charges, <strong>idle load balancers</strong>, <strong>unattached EBS volumes</strong>, old snapshots, and <strong>cross-AZ data transfer</strong>.</li>
+  <li>Right-size with Compute Optimizer, prefer <strong>Graviton</strong> (ARM) instances, buy <strong>Savings Plans</strong> for steady load, and use <strong>Spot</strong> for interruptible work.</li>
+  <li>Shut dev environments down at night. Better still, make them disposable with Terraform.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2>Security baseline checklist</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Root MFA on, root keys deleted, SSO for humans</li>
+  <li>CloudTrail on in all regions, logging to a locked-down bucket</li>
+  <li>S3 Block Public Access enabled at the account level</li>
+  <li>GuardDuty and Security Hub on; AWS Config for drift and compliance</li>
+  <li>Encryption at rest everywhere (EBS, S3, RDS default encryption)</li>
+  <li>Multiple accounts (dev / prod / security) under AWS Organizations, with SCP guardrails</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2>Certifications and next steps</h2>
+<p>A sensible path is <strong>Cloud Practitioner</strong> (optional, for fundamentals), then <strong>Solutions Architect – Associate</strong>, then <strong>DevOps Engineer – Professional</strong>. Hands-on work matters more than badges, though:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li>Build <a href="projects.html#terraform-aws">Project 04: a multi-AZ VPC and web tier with Terraform</a>.</li>
+  <li>Read <a href="blog-terraform-aws.html">Terraform on AWS</a> and <a href="blog-networking-fundamentals.html">Networking Fundamentals</a>.</li>
+  <li>Practise the <a href="interview.html#cloud">cloud interview questions</a>.</li>
+</ul>
diff --git a/_blog/posts/gitops-argocd.html b/_blog/posts/gitops-argocd.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ee605f3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/_blog/posts/gitops-argocd.html
@@ -0,0 +1,155 @@
+<p>In a traditional pipeline, CI runs <code>kubectl apply</code> or <code>helm upgrade</code> against your cluster. That means CI holds cluster-admin credentials, and nothing notices when someone changes the cluster by hand. <strong>GitOps</strong> flips this around: Git holds the desired state, and an agent <em>inside</em> the cluster continuously makes reality match it. Argo CD is the most popular such agent.</p>
+
+<h2>The four GitOps principles</h2>
+<ol>
+  <li><strong>Declarative:</strong> the whole system is described as desired state (YAML, Helm, Kustomize).</li>
+  <li><strong>Versioned and immutable:</strong> that state lives in Git, with full history and review.</li>
+  <li><strong>Pulled automatically:</strong> an agent pulls changes from Git. Nobody pushes to the cluster.</li>
+  <li><strong>Continuously reconciled:</strong> the agent detects drift and corrects it.</li>
+</ol>
+<p>What you get: every change is a reviewed commit, rollback is <code>git revert</code>, the audit trail comes for free, and CI no longer needs cluster credentials.</p>
+
+<h2>Repository layout</h2>
+<p>Keep application code and deployment configuration in <strong>separate repos</strong>, or at least separate folders. That way a config change doesn't trigger an app build, and access can be controlled separately.</p>
+<pre><code>gitops/
+├── apps/                    # one Argo CD Application per app+env (app-of-apps)
+│   ├── api-dev.yaml
+│   └── api-prod.yaml
+└── services/
+    └── api/
+        ├── base/            # shared manifests
+        │   ├── deployment.yaml
+        │   ├── service.yaml
+        │   └── kustomization.yaml
+        └── overlays/
+            ├── dev/         # replicas: 1, image tag bumped by CI
+            │   └── kustomization.yaml
+            └── prod/        # replicas: 3, promoted by PR
+                └── kustomization.yaml</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Install Argo CD</h2>
+<pre><code>kubectl create namespace argocd
+kubectl apply -n argocd \
+  -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/argoproj/argo-cd/stable/manifests/install.yaml
+
+# initial admin password
+kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret \
+  -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d; echo
+
+kubectl -n argocd port-forward svc/argocd-server 8080:443
+# open https://localhost:8080 (user: admin)</code></pre>
+<p>For anything beyond a lab, install it with the official Helm chart, pin the version, and set up SSO instead of the admin user.</p>
+
+<h2>Your first Application</h2>
+<p>An <code>Application</code> tells Argo CD where to read from and where to deploy to:</p>
+<pre><code>apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
+kind: Application
+metadata:
+  name: api-dev
+  namespace: argocd
+spec:
+  project: default
+  source:
+    repoURL: https://github.com/you/gitops
+    targetRevision: main
+    path: services/api/overlays/dev
+  destination:
+    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
+    namespace: api-dev
+  syncPolicy:
+    automated:
+      prune: true       # delete resources removed from Git
+      selfHeal: true    # revert manual changes in the cluster
+    syncOptions:
+      - CreateNamespace=true</code></pre>
+<p>Apply it once with <code>kubectl apply -f apps/api-dev.yaml</code>. From then on, every commit to <code>services/api/overlays/dev</code> is deployed automatically.</p>
+
+<h2>Sync status and health</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Synced / OutOfSync:</strong> does the live state match Git?</li>
+  <li><strong>Healthy / Progressing / Degraded:</strong> are the resources actually working? (e.g. whether the Deployment has rolled out all its replicas)</li>
+</ul>
+<pre><code>argocd app list
+argocd app get api-dev
+argocd app diff api-dev        # what would change
+argocd app sync api-dev        # manual sync (for apps without auto-sync)
+argocd app history api-dev</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Connecting CI to GitOps</h2>
+<p>CI's job ends at "build, test, push the image, and update Git". Argo CD handles the deployment.</p>
+<pre><code># .github/workflows/release.yml (in the APP repo, after the image push step)
+      - name: Bump image tag in gitops repo
+        env:
+          GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITOPS_TOKEN }}   # fine-grained token, gitops repo only
+        run: |
+          git clone https://x-access-token:${GH_TOKEN}@github.com/you/gitops.git
+          cd gitops/services/api/overlays/dev
+          kustomize edit set image api=ghcr.io/you/api:${{ github.sha }}
+          git -c user.name=ci -c user.email=ci@example.com commit -am "api: deploy ${{ github.sha }} to dev"
+          git push</code></pre>
+<p>Promoting to production is then a pull request that copies the tested tag into <code>overlays/prod</code>. A human reviews and merges it. If you'd rather not have CI write to Git at all, <strong>Argo CD Image Updater</strong> can watch the registry instead.</p>
+
+<h2>Scaling up: app-of-apps and ApplicationSets</h2>
+<p>Writing one Application per service per environment gets repetitive. Two patterns help:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>App of apps:</strong> one root Application points at the <code>apps/</code> folder and creates all the others.</li>
+  <li><strong>ApplicationSet:</strong> generates Applications from a template, e.g. one per folder or one per cluster.</li>
+</ul>
+<pre><code>apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1
+kind: ApplicationSet
+metadata:
+  name: services-dev
+  namespace: argocd
+spec:
+  goTemplate: true
+  generators:
+    - git:
+        repoURL: https://github.com/you/gitops
+        revision: main
+        directories:
+          - path: services/*/overlays/dev
+  template:
+    metadata:
+      name: '{{index .path.segments 1}}-dev'
+    spec:
+      project: default
+      source:
+        repoURL: https://github.com/you/gitops
+        targetRevision: main
+        path: '{{.path.path}}'
+      destination:
+        server: https://kubernetes.default.svc
+        namespace: '{{index .path.segments 1}}-dev'
+      syncPolicy:
+        automated: { prune: true, selfHeal: true }
+        syncOptions: [CreateNamespace=true]</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Secrets in GitOps</h2>
+<p>Never commit plain Kubernetes Secrets (base64 is not encryption). Choose one of:</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>External Secrets Operator:</strong> Git holds a reference, and the operator fetches the value from AWS Secrets Manager, Vault, etc. (the usual choice in the cloud).</li>
+  <li><strong>Sealed Secrets:</strong> encrypt with the cluster's public key, then commit the ciphertext.</li>
+  <li><strong>SOPS</strong> with KMS/age, decrypted by a plugin at sync time.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2>Progressive delivery with Argo Rollouts</h2>
+<p>Argo CD deploys whatever is in Git. <strong>Argo Rollouts</strong> controls <em>how</em> it rolls out: canary steps, traffic shifting, and automated analysis against Prometheus metrics that aborts and rolls back on its own.</p>
+<pre><code>strategy:
+  canary:
+    steps:
+      - setWeight: 10
+      - pause: { duration: 5m }
+      - analysis:
+          templates: [{ templateName: success-rate }]
+      - setWeight: 50
+      - pause: { duration: 10m }</code></pre>
+
+<div class="article-callout">
+  <p><strong>Common pitfall:</strong> with <code>selfHeal</code> on, the HPA and Argo CD fight over <code>spec.replicas</code>. Remove <code>replicas</code> from the Deployment manifest when you use an HPA, or add an <code>ignoreDifferences</code> entry for that field.</p>
+</div>
+
+<h2>Next steps</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Build <a href="projects.html#gitops-argocd">Project 07: GitOps Delivery with Argo CD</a>.</li>
+  <li>Review <a href="blog-k8s-beginners.html">Kubernetes for Beginners</a> and <a href="blog-cicd-github-actions.html">CI/CD with GitHub Actions</a>.</li>
+</ul>
diff --git a/_blog/posts/linux-for-devops.html b/_blog/posts/linux-for-devops.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ca9b7bb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/_blog/posts/linux-for-devops.html
@@ -0,0 +1,179 @@
+<p>Almost everything in DevOps runs on Linux: CI runners, containers, Kubernetes nodes and cloud VMs. You don't need to be a kernel hacker, but you do need to be fast and confident at the command line. This guide covers the commands and concepts you'll use every week, in the order you'll need them.</p>
+
+<div class="article-callout">
+  <p><strong>Practise as you read:</strong> run an Ubuntu VM with <code>multipass launch --name lab</code>, use WSL2 on Windows, or run a throwaway container: <code>docker run -it --rm ubuntu:24.04 bash</code>.</p>
+</div>
+
+<h2>The filesystem layout</h2>
+<p>Linux has a single tree starting at <code>/</code>. The directories you'll visit most:</p>
+<table>
+  <thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>What lives there</th></tr></thead>
+  <tbody>
+    <tr><td><code>/etc</code></td><td>System and service configuration (<code>/etc/nginx</code>, <code>/etc/ssh/sshd_config</code>)</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>/var/log</code></td><td>Log files (and <code>journalctl</code> for systemd services)</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>/var/lib</code></td><td>Application state, e.g. <code>/var/lib/docker</code></td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>/home</code>, <code>/root</code></td><td>User home directories</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>/usr/bin</code>, <code>/usr/local/bin</code></td><td>Installed programs</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>/tmp</code></td><td>Temporary files, often cleared on reboot</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>/proc</code>, <code>/sys</code></td><td>Virtual filesystems exposing kernel and process information</td></tr>
+  </tbody>
+</table>
+
+<h2>Navigating and finding things</h2>
+<pre><code>pwd                         # where am I?
+ls -lah                     # long list, hidden files, human sizes
+cd -                        # jump back to the previous directory
+find /var/log -name "*.log" -mtime -1      # logs modified in the last day
+find . -type f -size +100M                 # big files under here
+which nginx; type ll        # where does a command come from?
+tree -L 2                   # directory overview (apt install tree)</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Reading and searching text</h2>
+<p>Most DevOps debugging is reading logs and config. Learn these until they're muscle memory:</p>
+<pre><code>less /var/log/syslog        # page through (/ to search, G for end, q to quit)
+tail -f /var/log/nginx/access.log          # follow a log live
+tail -n 100 app.log | grep -i error
+grep -rn "listen" /etc/nginx/               # recursive, with line numbers
+grep -v '^#' sshd_config | grep -v '^$'     # strip comments and blank lines
+
+# count requests per status code in an nginx access log
+awk '{print $9}' access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
+
+# top 10 client IPs
+awk '{print $1}' access.log | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head
+
+# find and replace in place (keep a .bak backup)
+sed -i.bak 's/old-host/new-host/g' config.yaml</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Users, groups and permissions</h2>
+<p>Every file has an owner, a group and three permission sets: <strong>user</strong>, <strong>group</strong> and <strong>others</strong>. Each set has read (4), write (2) and execute (1).</p>
+<pre><code>ls -l deploy.sh
+# -rwxr-x--- 1 deploy ops 1.2K deploy.sh
+#  └u┘└g┘└o┘   owner  group
+
+chmod 750 deploy.sh         # rwx for owner, r-x for group, nothing for others
+chmod +x script.sh          # add execute for everyone
+chown deploy:ops deploy.sh  # change owner and group
+sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash deploy
+sudo usermod -aG docker deploy   # add to a group (log out and in to apply)
+id deploy                    # show uid, gid and groups</code></pre>
+
+<div class="article-callout warn">
+  <p><strong>Never fix a permission error with <code>chmod 777</code>.</strong> Work out which user the process runs as (<code>ps -o user= -p &lt;pid&gt;</code>) and give <em>that</em> user the minimum access it needs.</p>
+</div>
+
+<h2>Processes and resources</h2>
+<pre><code>ps aux --sort=-%mem | head   # top memory consumers
+top                          # or htop: live view (press P for CPU, M for memory)
+pgrep -a python              # find processes by name
+kill &lt;pid&gt;                   # ask nicely (SIGTERM)
+kill -9 &lt;pid&gt;                # force (SIGKILL), last resort
+free -h                      # memory (look at "available", not "free")
+df -h                        # disk space per filesystem
+du -sh /var/* | sort -h      # what's using the space?
+uptime                       # load averages for 1, 5 and 15 minutes</code></pre>
+<p>A load average higher than your CPU count (<code>nproc</code>) means work is queuing. If the load is high but CPU usage is low, processes are usually waiting on disk or network I/O.</p>
+
+<h2>Services with systemd</h2>
+<p>Modern distributions use <strong>systemd</strong> to start and supervise services.</p>
+<pre><code>systemctl status nginx
+sudo systemctl restart nginx
+sudo systemctl enable --now nginx      # start now and on every boot
+systemctl list-units --failed
+journalctl -u nginx -f                 # follow a service's logs
+journalctl -u nginx --since "1 hour ago" -p err</code></pre>
+<p>Running your own app as a service takes one small unit file:</p>
+<pre><code># /etc/systemd/system/myapp.service
+[Unit]
+Description=My API
+After=network-online.target
+Wants=network-online.target
+
+[Service]
+User=myapp
+WorkingDirectory=/opt/myapp
+EnvironmentFile=/etc/myapp/env
+ExecStart=/opt/myapp/bin/server --port 8080
+Restart=on-failure
+RestartSec=5
+
+[Install]
+WantedBy=multi-user.target</code></pre>
+<pre><code>sudo systemctl daemon-reload
+sudo systemctl enable --now myapp</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Networking from the shell</h2>
+<pre><code>ip a                          # interfaces and IPs
+ip route                      # routing table
+ss -tlnp                      # which processes listen on which TCP ports
+curl -v https://example.com   # full request/response, including TLS
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code} %{time_total}s\n" https://example.com
+dig +short example.com        # DNS lookup
+nc -zv db.internal 5432       # is the port reachable?</code></pre>
+<p>For more on how these fit together, read <a href="blog-networking-fundamentals.html">Networking Fundamentals for DevOps</a>.</p>
+
+<h2>SSH like a professional</h2>
+<pre><code>ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "you@example.com"
+ssh-copy-id deploy@server
+ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 deploy@server
+
+# ~/.ssh/config: then just run "ssh web1"
+Host web1
+  HostName 203.0.113.10
+  User deploy
+  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
+
+# copy files
+scp app.tar.gz web1:/tmp/
+rsync -avz --delete ./dist/ web1:/var/www/app/
+
+# reach a private database through a bastion
+ssh -L 5432:db.internal:5432 web1</code></pre>
+<p>On servers, disable password logins (<code>PasswordAuthentication no</code>) and root login (<code>PermitRootLogin no</code>) in <code>/etc/ssh/sshd_config</code>.</p>
+
+<h2>Bash scripting essentials</h2>
+<p>Start every script with this header. It catches most of the bugs that make automation dangerous:</p>
+<pre><code>#!/usr/bin/env bash
+set -euo pipefail      # exit on error, on unset vars, and on failures inside pipes
+IFS=$'\n\t'
+
+log() { printf '%s %s\n' "$(date -u +%FT%TZ)" "$*" &gt;&amp;2; }
+
+usage() { echo "usage: $0 &lt;environment&gt;" &gt;&amp;2; exit 1; }
+[[ $# -eq 1 ]] || usage
+ENV=$1
+
+case "$ENV" in
+  dev|staging|prod) ;;
+  *) usage ;;
+esac
+
+tmp=$(mktemp -d)
+trap 'rm -rf "$tmp"' EXIT          # always clean up, even on failure
+
+for svc in api worker; do
+  log "deploying $svc to $ENV"
+  if ! curl -fsS "https://$svc.$ENV.example.com/health" &gt;/dev/null; then
+    log "health check failed for $svc"
+    exit 1
+  fi
+done</code></pre>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Quote your variables</strong> (<code>"$var"</code>) so spaces don't split them.</li>
+  <li>Use <code>[[ ... ]]</code> for tests, and <code>$(...)</code> for command substitution.</li>
+  <li>Run <code>shellcheck script.sh</code>. It finds real bugs in seconds.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2>Scheduling: cron and systemd timers</h2>
+<pre><code>crontab -e
+# m  h  dom mon dow  command
+*/5  *  *   *   *    /opt/scripts/healthcheck.sh &gt;&gt; /var/log/health.log 2&gt;&amp;1
+0    2  *   *   *    /opt/scripts/backup.sh</code></pre>
+<p>systemd timers are the modern alternative. Their logs go to the journal, they can catch up on runs missed while the machine was off, and <code>systemctl list-timers</code> shows when each one next runs.</p>
+
+<h2>What to practise next</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Build <a href="projects.html#linux-automation">Project 01: Linux Server Health &amp; Backup Automation</a>.</li>
+  <li>Keep the <a href="cheatsheets.html#linux">Linux cheatsheet</a> open while you work.</li>
+  <li>When something breaks, work through the <a href="troubleshooting.html#linux">Linux troubleshooting playbooks</a>.</li>
+</ul>
diff --git a/_blog/posts/networking-fundamentals.html b/_blog/posts/networking-fundamentals.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c5a7735
--- /dev/null
+++ b/_blog/posts/networking-fundamentals.html
@@ -0,0 +1,104 @@
+<p>Networking problems often look like application bugs: timeouts, 502s, "connection refused". Engineers who understand what happens between client and server debug them in minutes instead of hours. This guide covers the networking you actually need for cloud and Kubernetes work.</p>
+
+<h2>The layers that matter</h2>
+<p>You don't need to memorise the whole OSI model, but these four layers come up constantly:</p>
+<table>
+  <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it does</th><th>You meet it as</th></tr></thead>
+  <tbody>
+    <tr><td>L3 Network</td><td>IP addressing and routing between networks</td><td>VPCs, subnets, route tables, <code>ip route</code></td></tr>
+    <tr><td>L4 Transport</td><td>TCP (reliable) and UDP (fast) between ports</td><td>Security groups, NLBs, <code>ss -tlnp</code></td></tr>
+    <tr><td>L7 Application</td><td>HTTP, DNS, gRPC</td><td>ALBs, Ingress, API gateways, <code>curl</code></td></tr>
+    <tr><td>TLS (between L4 and L7)</td><td>Encryption and identity</td><td>Certificates, HTTPS, mTLS</td></tr>
+  </tbody>
+</table>
+
+<h2>IP addresses and CIDR</h2>
+<p>An IPv4 address is 32 bits, written as four octets (<code>10.0.1.25</code>). <strong>CIDR</strong> notation says how many leading bits identify the network:</p>
+<table>
+  <thead><tr><th>CIDR</th><th>Addresses</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
+  <tbody>
+    <tr><td><code>10.0.0.0/16</code></td><td>65,536</td><td>A whole VPC</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>10.0.1.0/24</code></td><td>256</td><td>One subnet</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>10.0.1.0/28</code></td><td>16</td><td>A tiny subnet</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>203.0.113.7/32</code></td><td>1</td><td>A single host in a firewall rule</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>0.0.0.0/0</code></td><td>Everything</td><td>The default route, or "anywhere"</td></tr>
+  </tbody>
+</table>
+<p>The formula is 2<sup>(32 − prefix)</sup>. The private ranges (RFC 1918) are <code>10.0.0.0/8</code>, <code>172.16.0.0/12</code> and <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>.</p>
+
+<div class="article-callout">
+  <p><strong>Plan for growth:</strong> give each VPC a different, non-overlapping range (e.g. dev <code>10.10.0.0/16</code>, prod <code>10.20.0.0/16</code>). Overlapping ranges make VPC peering and VPNs impossible later. Remember that Kubernetes pod and service CIDRs need room too.</p>
+</div>
+
+<h2>TCP, UDP and ports</h2>
+<p><strong>TCP</strong> sets up a connection with a three-way handshake (SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK), then guarantees ordered, reliable delivery. <strong>UDP</strong> just sends datagrams, which is faster with no guarantees. DNS mostly uses UDP. HTTP/3 (QUIC) runs over UDP and builds its own reliability on top.</p>
+<p>Ports you should recognise: 22 (SSH), 53 (DNS), 80/443 (HTTP/HTTPS), 3306 (MySQL), 5432 (PostgreSQL), 6379 (Redis), 6443 (Kubernetes API), 9090 (Prometheus), 3000 (Grafana).</p>
+
+<h2>DNS: how names become IPs</h2>
+<p>When you resolve <code>api.example.com</code>: the OS asks its configured resolver, which (if it hasn't cached the answer) asks a root server, then the <code>.com</code> servers, then <code>example.com</code>'s authoritative name servers. The answer is cached for its <strong>TTL</strong>.</p>
+<pre><code>dig api.example.com                 # full answer, including TTL
+dig +short api.example.com
+dig api.example.com @1.1.1.1        # ask a specific resolver
+dig +trace api.example.com          # follow the delegation chain
+dig -x 203.0.113.10                 # reverse lookup</code></pre>
+<p>Common record types: <code>A</code>/<code>AAAA</code> (name to IP), <code>CNAME</code> (alias), <code>MX</code> (mail), <code>TXT</code> (verification, SPF), <code>NS</code> (delegation). In Kubernetes, every Service gets a name like <code>my-svc.my-namespace.svc.cluster.local</code>.</p>
+
+<h2>HTTP and HTTPS</h2>
+<pre><code>curl -v https://example.com/health
+# * Connected to example.com (93.184.215.14) port 443     ← TCP ok
+# * SSL connection using TLSv1.3                           ← TLS ok
+# &gt; GET /health HTTP/2                                     ← request
+# &lt; HTTP/2 200                                             ← response
+
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "dns:%{time_namelookup} connect:%{time_connect} tls:%{time_appconnect} total:%{time_total}\n" https://example.com</code></pre>
+<p>That second command breaks latency down by phase, so you can tell whether the slowness is DNS, the network, the TLS handshake or the server itself.</p>
+<p>Know your status codes: <strong>2xx</strong> success, <strong>3xx</strong> redirect, <strong>4xx</strong> client error (401 unauthenticated, 403 forbidden, 404 not found, 429 rate limited) and <strong>5xx</strong> server error. From a load balancer, <strong>502</strong> means the backend gave a bad response or none at all, <strong>503</strong> means no healthy backends, and <strong>504</strong> means the backend timed out.</p>
+
+<h2>TLS and certificates</h2>
+<p>TLS encrypts traffic and proves the server's identity. The server presents a certificate for its hostname, signed by a CA that the client trusts. Most TLS errors come down to three things: the certificate has expired, the chain is incomplete, or the hostname isn't in the certificate's SAN list.</p>
+<pre><code>openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -servername example.com &lt;/dev/null \
+  | openssl x509 -noout -subject -issuer -dates -ext subjectAltName</code></pre>
+<p>Automate certificates with <strong>cert-manager</strong> (Kubernetes), <strong>ACM</strong> (AWS) or <strong>certbot</strong>, and alert well before they expire.</p>
+
+<h2>Load balancers and proxies</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>L4 load balancers</strong> (AWS NLB) forward TCP/UDP connections. They are very fast and keep the client IP.</li>
+  <li><strong>L7 load balancers</strong> (AWS ALB, Nginx, Envoy, Ingress controllers) understand HTTP. They can route by host and path, terminate TLS, retry, and add headers like <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>.</li>
+  <li><strong>Reverse proxies</strong> sit in front of apps. <strong>Forward proxies</strong> sit in front of clients (corporate egress).</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Health checks decide which backends receive traffic, so a wrong health-check path is one of the most common causes of 503s.</p>
+
+<h2>NAT, gateways and firewalls in the cloud</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Internet Gateway:</strong> lets a public subnet talk to the internet in both directions.</li>
+  <li><strong>NAT Gateway:</strong> lets private subnets make <em>outbound</em> connections without being reachable from outside.</li>
+  <li><strong>Security groups:</strong> stateful, allow-only rules attached to instances and ENIs.</li>
+  <li><strong>Network ACLs:</strong> stateless rules that apply to whole subnets.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>See <a href="blog-aws-core-services.html">AWS Core Services for DevOps Engineers</a> for how these fit into a VPC design.</p>
+
+<h2>A debugging checklist</h2>
+<p>Work from the bottom up and stop at the first layer that fails:</p>
+<pre><code># 1. Does the name resolve to the IP you expect?
+dig +short db.internal
+# 2. Is there a route?
+ip route get 10.20.3.15
+# 3. Is the port reachable? (refused = nothing listening; timeout = firewall)
+nc -zv 10.20.3.15 5432
+# 4. On the server: is it listening, and on which address?
+sudo ss -tlnp | grep 5432      # 127.0.0.1:5432 means local-only!
+# 5. Does TLS succeed?
+openssl s_client -connect host:443 -servername host
+# 6. What does the application say?
+curl -v http://host:8080/health</code></pre>
+
+<div class="article-callout">
+  <p><strong>Connection refused vs timeout:</strong> "refused" means a host actively rejected you, so the network path works but nothing is listening on that port. A "timeout" means packets are being silently dropped, almost always by a security group, NACL or firewall rule.</p>
+</div>
+
+<h2>Next steps</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Design a VPC in <a href="projects.html#terraform-aws">Project 04: Terraform an AWS Network &amp; Web Tier</a>.</li>
+  <li>Practise the <a href="troubleshooting.html#connection-refused-vs-connection-timed-out">connection</a> and <a href="troubleshooting.html#dns-resolution-failures">DNS</a> playbooks.</li>
+  <li>Test yourself with the <a href="interview.html#networking">networking interview questions</a>.</li>
+</ul>
diff --git a/_blog/posts/sre-fundamentals.html b/_blog/posts/sre-fundamentals.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..2c2bdcc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/_blog/posts/sre-fundamentals.html
@@ -0,0 +1,115 @@
+<p>Site Reliability Engineering (SRE) is what happens when you treat operations as a software problem. It gives teams a shared, numerical answer to the question that causes most dev-vs-ops arguments: <em>how reliable is reliable enough?</em> This guide covers SLIs, SLOs, error budgets, alerting, incidents and toil, with formulas and queries you can use straight away.</p>
+
+<h2>Why 100% is the wrong target</h2>
+<p>Users can't tell 99.99% from 100%: their own Wi-Fi and phones fail far more often than that. Each extra "nine" costs a lot more engineering and slows feature work. SRE picks a target that keeps users happy and spends the rest of the reliability "budget" on shipping.</p>
+<table>
+  <thead><tr><th>Availability</th><th>Allowed downtime per 30 days</th></tr></thead>
+  <tbody>
+    <tr><td>99%</td><td>7 h 12 min</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>99.5%</td><td>3 h 36 min</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>99.9%</td><td>43 min</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>99.95%</td><td>21.6 min</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>99.99%</td><td>4.3 min</td></tr>
+  </tbody>
+</table>
+
+<h2>SLI, SLO, SLA</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>SLI (indicator):</strong> a measurement of user experience, usually <em>good events ÷ valid events</em>. Example: the proportion of HTTP requests that return non-5xx in under 300 ms.</li>
+  <li><strong>SLO (objective):</strong> the target for an SLI over a window. Example: 99.9% of requests are good, over 30 days.</li>
+  <li><strong>SLA (agreement):</strong> a contract with customers, with consequences. Always looser than your SLO, so you get warned before you breach it.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Good SLIs measure what <strong>users</strong> feel, as close to them as possible (at the load balancer, or with synthetic probes), not CPU or memory.</p>
+
+<h2>Error budgets</h2>
+<p>The error budget is <code>1 − SLO</code>. With a 99.9% SLO and 10 million requests a month, you can "spend" 10,000 failed requests.</p>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Budget remaining:</strong> ship features, run experiments, take calculated risks.</li>
+  <li><strong>Budget exhausted:</strong> freeze risky launches and spend engineering time on reliability until it recovers.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Agree this <strong>error budget policy</strong> with product owners <em>in advance</em>. Then reliability decisions are driven by data rather than by whoever argues loudest during an outage.</p>
+
+<h2>Measuring SLIs with Prometheus</h2>
+<pre><code># Availability SLI: share of requests that were NOT 5xx over 30 days
+sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="api", code!~"5.."}[30d]))
+  /
+sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="api"}[30d]))
+
+# Latency SLI: share of requests faster than 300 ms
+# (needs a histogram bucket at le="0.3")
+sum(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{job="api", le="0.3"}[30d]))
+  /
+sum(rate(http_request_duration_seconds_count{job="api"}[30d]))
+
+# Error budget remaining (1 = untouched, 0 = spent), for a 99.9% SLO
+1 - (
+  (1 - (sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="api", code!~"5.."}[30d]))
+        / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="api"}[30d]))))
+  / (1 - 0.999)
+)</code></pre>
+<p>In practice, use recording rules, or a generator like <strong>Sloth</strong> or <strong>Pyrra</strong>, so these long-window queries stay cheap.</p>
+
+<h2>Alert on burn rate, not thresholds</h2>
+<p><strong>Burn rate</strong> is how fast you're spending the budget. A burn rate of 1 uses exactly the whole budget over the SLO window; 14.4 uses 2% of a 30-day budget in one hour. The <strong>multi-window, multi-burn-rate</strong> approach from the Google SRE Workbook pages for fast burns and opens tickets for slow ones:</p>
+<table>
+  <thead><tr><th>Severity</th><th>Burn rate</th><th>Long window</th><th>Short window</th><th>Budget consumed</th></tr></thead>
+  <tbody>
+    <tr><td>Page</td><td>14.4</td><td>1 h</td><td>5 min</td><td>2%</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Page</td><td>6</td><td>6 h</td><td>30 min</td><td>5%</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Ticket</td><td>1</td><td>3 days</td><td>6 h</td><td>10%</td></tr>
+  </tbody>
+</table>
+<pre><code># Fast-burn page for a 99.9% SLO (error budget = 0.001)
+(
+  sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="api",code=~"5.."}[1h]))
+    / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="api"}[1h])) &gt; (14.4 * 0.001)
+)
+and
+(
+  sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="api",code=~"5.."}[5m]))
+    / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="api"}[5m])) &gt; (14.4 * 0.001)
+)</code></pre>
+<p>The short window makes the alert resolve quickly once the problem stops. The long window stops brief blips from paging anyone.</p>
+
+<h2>Incident response</h2>
+<ol>
+  <li><strong>Detect:</strong> from SLO alerts, ideally before customers notice.</li>
+  <li><strong>Declare and assign roles:</strong> an <em>Incident Commander</em> coordinates, an <em>Ops lead</em> fixes, and a <em>Comms lead</em> updates stakeholders and the status page.</li>
+  <li><strong>Mitigate first:</strong> roll back, fail over, scale up, disable a feature flag. Root cause can wait.</li>
+  <li><strong>Communicate</strong> on a fixed cadence (e.g. every 30 minutes), even if nothing has changed.</li>
+  <li><strong>Resolve</strong> and confirm the SLIs have recovered.</li>
+  <li><strong>Learn:</strong> hold a blameless postmortem.</li>
+</ol>
+
+<h2>Blameless postmortems</h2>
+<p>People rarely cause incidents on their own; systems allow them. A postmortem asks <em>how the system let this happen</em>, not <em>who did it</em>. If people fear blame, they hide information and the same failure comes back.</p>
+<pre><code>## Incident: API 5xx spike, 2026-03-14
+Impact:     18% of requests failed for 42 min; ~3,100 users affected; 31% of monthly error budget used
+Detection:  Fast-burn SLO page at 14:07 (6 min after start)
+Timeline:   14:01 deploy v2.31 → 14:07 page → 14:15 rollback started → 14:43 recovered
+Root cause: Connection pool size cut from 50 to 5 in a config refactor; no load test caught it
+What went well:     Alert fired fast; rollback was one command
+What went poorly:   Rollback took 28 min because the old image had been garbage-collected
+Action items:
+  - [ ] Add a config validation test for pool sizes          (owner: @dev-a, due 03-21)
+  - [ ] Keep the last 10 release images in the registry       (owner: @ops-b, due 03-18)
+  - [ ] Add a pre-prod load test to the release pipeline      (owner: @sre-c, due 04-01)</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Toil and automation</h2>
+<p><strong>Toil</strong> is manual, repetitive, automatable work that grows with the service and has no lasting value: restarting a stuck job, rotating a certificate by hand, approving routine access requests. Google SRE teams cap toil at about 50% of their time and use the rest to engineer it away.</p>
+<p>A simple habit: keep a toil log for two weeks, rank the entries by <em>time × frequency</em>, and automate the top one.</p>
+
+<h2>Other SRE practices worth knowing</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Capacity planning:</strong> forecast from growth trends; load-test before launches.</li>
+  <li><strong>Chaos engineering:</strong> inject failures in a controlled way (LitmusChaos, Chaos Mesh, AWS FIS) to test your assumptions.</li>
+  <li><strong>Release engineering:</strong> canaries, feature flags and fast rollback. See <a href="blog-gitops-argocd.html">GitOps with Argo CD</a>.</li>
+  <li><strong>DORA metrics:</strong> measure delivery performance alongside reliability. See <a href="blog-dora-metrics.html">DORA Metrics Explained</a>.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2>Next steps</h2>
