+    <h1>Networking Fundamentals for DevOps Engineers</h1>
+    <p class="post-lede">IP and CIDR, TCP vs UDP, DNS, HTTP status codes, TLS, load balancers and NAT, plus a layer-by-layer checklist for debugging connectivity.</p>
+    <div class="post-meta">
+      <span>🌐 Networking</span>
+      <span>📅 <time datetime="2026-10-18">18 Oct 2026</time></span>
+      <span>⏱ 5 min read</span>
+      <span>📶 Beginner</span>
+    </div>
+    <div class="post-tags"><span class="post-tag">Networking</span><span class="post-tag">DNS</span><span class="post-tag">TLS</span><span class="post-tag">Beginner</span></div>
+  </div>
+</header>
+
+<div class="container">
+  <div class="post-layout">
+    <article class="post-content">
+<p>Networking problems often look like application bugs: timeouts, 502s, "connection refused". Engineers who understand what happens between client and server debug them in minutes instead of hours. This guide covers the networking you actually need for cloud and Kubernetes work.</p>
+
+<h2 id="the-layers-that-matter">The layers that matter</h2>
+<p>You don't need to memorise the whole OSI model, but these four layers come up constantly:</p>
+<table>
+  <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it does</th><th>You meet it as</th></tr></thead>
+  <tbody>
+    <tr><td>L3 Network</td><td>IP addressing and routing between networks</td><td>VPCs, subnets, route tables, <code>ip route</code></td></tr>
+    <tr><td>L4 Transport</td><td>TCP (reliable) and UDP (fast) between ports</td><td>Security groups, NLBs, <code>ss -tlnp</code></td></tr>
+    <tr><td>L7 Application</td><td>HTTP, DNS, gRPC</td><td>ALBs, Ingress, API gateways, <code>curl</code></td></tr>
+    <tr><td>TLS (between L4 and L7)</td><td>Encryption and identity</td><td>Certificates, HTTPS, mTLS</td></tr>
+  </tbody>
+</table>
+
+<h2 id="ip-addresses-and-cidr">IP addresses and CIDR</h2>
+<p>An IPv4 address is 32 bits, written as four octets (<code>10.0.1.25</code>). <strong>CIDR</strong> notation says how many leading bits identify the network:</p>
+<table>
+  <thead><tr><th>CIDR</th><th>Addresses</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
+  <tbody>
+    <tr><td><code>10.0.0.0/16</code></td><td>65,536</td><td>A whole VPC</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>10.0.1.0/24</code></td><td>256</td><td>One subnet</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>10.0.1.0/28</code></td><td>16</td><td>A tiny subnet</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>203.0.113.7/32</code></td><td>1</td><td>A single host in a firewall rule</td></tr>
+    <tr><td><code>0.0.0.0/0</code></td><td>Everything</td><td>The default route, or "anywhere"</td></tr>
+  </tbody>
+</table>
+<p>The formula is 2<sup>(32 − prefix)</sup>. The private ranges (RFC 1918) are <code>10.0.0.0/8</code>, <code>172.16.0.0/12</code> and <code>192.168.0.0/16</code>.</p>
+
+<div class="article-callout">
+  <p><strong>Plan for growth:</strong> give each VPC a different, non-overlapping range (e.g. dev <code>10.10.0.0/16</code>, prod <code>10.20.0.0/16</code>). Overlapping ranges make VPC peering and VPNs impossible later. Remember that Kubernetes pod and service CIDRs need room too.</p>
+</div>
+
+<h2 id="tcp-udp-and-ports">TCP, UDP and ports</h2>
+<p><strong>TCP</strong> sets up a connection with a three-way handshake (SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK), then guarantees ordered, reliable delivery. <strong>UDP</strong> just sends datagrams, which is faster with no guarantees. DNS mostly uses UDP. HTTP/3 (QUIC) runs over UDP and builds its own reliability on top.</p>
+<p>Ports you should recognise: 22 (SSH), 53 (DNS), 80/443 (HTTP/HTTPS), 3306 (MySQL), 5432 (PostgreSQL), 6379 (Redis), 6443 (Kubernetes API), 9090 (Prometheus), 3000 (Grafana).</p>
+
+<h2 id="dns-how-names-become-ips">DNS: how names become IPs</h2>
+<p>When you resolve <code>api.example.com</code>: the OS asks its configured resolver, which (if it hasn't cached the answer) asks a root server, then the <code>.com</code> servers, then <code>example.com</code>'s authoritative name servers. The answer is cached for its <strong>TTL</strong>.</p>
+<pre><code>dig api.example.com                 # full answer, including TTL
+dig +short api.example.com
+dig api.example.com @1.1.1.1        # ask a specific resolver
+dig +trace api.example.com          # follow the delegation chain
+dig -x 203.0.113.10                 # reverse lookup</code></pre>
+<p>Common record types: <code>A</code>/<code>AAAA</code> (name to IP), <code>CNAME</code> (alias), <code>MX</code> (mail), <code>TXT</code> (verification, SPF), <code>NS</code> (delegation). In Kubernetes, every Service gets a name like <code>my-svc.my-namespace.svc.cluster.local</code>.</p>
+
+<h2 id="http-and-https">HTTP and HTTPS</h2>
+<pre><code>curl -v https://example.com/health
+# * Connected to example.com (93.184.215.14) port 443     ← TCP ok
+# * SSL connection using TLSv1.3                           ← TLS ok
+# &gt; GET /health HTTP/2                                     ← request
+# &lt; HTTP/2 200                                             ← response
+
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "dns:%{time_namelookup} connect:%{time_connect} tls:%{time_appconnect} total:%{time_total}\n" https://example.com</code></pre>
+<p>That second command breaks latency down by phase, so you can tell whether the slowness is DNS, the network, the TLS handshake or the server itself.</p>
+<p>Know your status codes: <strong>2xx</strong> success, <strong>3xx</strong> redirect, <strong>4xx</strong> client error (401 unauthenticated, 403 forbidden, 404 not found, 429 rate limited) and <strong>5xx</strong> server error. From a load balancer, <strong>502</strong> means the backend gave a bad response or none at all, <strong>503</strong> means no healthy backends, and <strong>504</strong> means the backend timed out.</p>
+
+<h2 id="tls-and-certificates">TLS and certificates</h2>
+<p>TLS encrypts traffic and proves the server's identity. The server presents a certificate for its hostname, signed by a CA that the client trusts. Most TLS errors come down to three things: the certificate has expired, the chain is incomplete, or the hostname isn't in the certificate's SAN list.</p>
+<pre><code>openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -servername example.com &lt;/dev/null \
+  | openssl x509 -noout -subject -issuer -dates -ext subjectAltName</code></pre>
+<p>Automate certificates with <strong>cert-manager</strong> (Kubernetes), <strong>ACM</strong> (AWS) or <strong>certbot</strong>, and alert well before they expire.</p>
+
+<h2 id="load-balancers-and-proxies">Load balancers and proxies</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>L4 load balancers</strong> (AWS NLB) forward TCP/UDP connections. They are very fast and keep the client IP.</li>
+  <li><strong>L7 load balancers</strong> (AWS ALB, Nginx, Envoy, Ingress controllers) understand HTTP. They can route by host and path, terminate TLS, retry, and add headers like <code>X-Forwarded-For</code>.</li>
+  <li><strong>Reverse proxies</strong> sit in front of apps. <strong>Forward proxies</strong> sit in front of clients (corporate egress).</li>
+</ul>
+<p>Health checks decide which backends receive traffic, so a wrong health-check path is one of the most common causes of 503s.</p>
+
+<h2 id="nat-gateways-and-firewalls-in-the-cloud">NAT, gateways and firewalls in the cloud</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Internet Gateway:</strong> lets a public subnet talk to the internet in both directions.</li>
+  <li><strong>NAT Gateway:</strong> lets private subnets make <em>outbound</em> connections without being reachable from outside.</li>
+  <li><strong>Security groups:</strong> stateful, allow-only rules attached to instances and ENIs.</li>
+  <li><strong>Network ACLs:</strong> stateless rules that apply to whole subnets.</li>
+</ul>
+<p>See <a href="blog-aws-core-services.html">AWS Core Services for DevOps Engineers</a> for how these fit into a VPC design.</p>
+
+<h2 id="a-debugging-checklist">A debugging checklist</h2>
+<p>Work from the bottom up and stop at the first layer that fails:</p>
+<pre><code># 1. Does the name resolve to the IP you expect?
+dig +short db.internal
+# 2. Is there a route?
+ip route get 10.20.3.15
+# 3. Is the port reachable? (refused = nothing listening; timeout = firewall)
+nc -zv 10.20.3.15 5432
+# 4. On the server: is it listening, and on which address?
+sudo ss -tlnp | grep 5432      # 127.0.0.1:5432 means local-only!
+# 5. Does TLS succeed?
+openssl s_client -connect host:443 -servername host
+# 6. What does the application say?
+curl -v http://host:8080/health</code></pre>
+
+<div class="article-callout">
+  <p><strong>Connection refused vs timeout:</strong> "refused" means a host actively rejected you, so the network path works but nothing is listening on that port. A "timeout" means packets are being silently dropped, almost always by a security group, NACL or firewall rule.</p>
+</div>
+
