diff --git a/_blog/posts/cicd-github-actions.html b/_blog/posts/cicd-github-actions.html
index 61e28a1..9191f6b 100644
--- a/_blog/posts/cicd-github-actions.html
+++ b/_blog/posts/cicd-github-actions.html
@@ -68,3 +68,77 @@ jobs:
   <li><strong>Runner</strong> — the machine that executes your workflow</li>
   <li><strong>Secrets</strong> — encrypted variables for credentials</li>
 </ul>
+
+<h2>Speed it up: caching and parallel jobs</h2>
+<p>Jobs run in parallel unless you link them with <code>needs:</code>. A <strong>matrix</strong> runs the same job across versions or platforms:</p>
+<pre><code>jobs:
+  test:
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    strategy:
+      matrix:
+        node: [20, 22]
+    steps:
+      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
+      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4
+        with:
+          node-version: ${{ matrix.node }}
+          cache: npm          # caches ~/.npm keyed on package-lock.json
+      - run: npm ci
+      - run: npm test</code></pre>
+<p>Docker builds can reuse layers between runs through the GitHub Actions cache:</p>
+<pre><code>      - uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v3
+      - uses: docker/build-push-action@v6
+        with:
+          push: true
+          tags: ghcr.io/you/app:${{ github.sha }}
+          cache-from: type=gha
+          cache-to: type=gha,mode=max</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Least-privilege permissions</h2>
+<p>Every workflow gets a <code>GITHUB_TOKEN</code>. Restrict it to what the workflow needs:</p>
+<pre><code>permissions:
+  contents: read
+  packages: write      # only if pushing to GHCR
+  id-token: write      # only if using OIDC to a cloud</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Deploy to the cloud without stored keys (OIDC)</h2>
+<p>Instead of saving AWS access keys as secrets, let GitHub issue a short-lived token that AWS trusts:</p>
+<pre><code>  deploy:
+    needs: docker
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    environment: production         # can require manual approval
+    permissions:
+      id-token: write
+      contents: read
+    steps:
+      - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4
+        with:
+          role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/github-deploy
+          aws-region: eu-west-1
+      - run: aws sts get-caller-identity</code></pre>
+<p>In AWS, create an IAM OIDC provider for <code>token.actions.githubusercontent.com</code>, and a role whose trust policy only allows <code>repo:you/app:environment:production</code>.</p>
+
+<h2>Environments and approvals</h2>
+<p>GitHub <strong>Environments</strong> (Settings → Environments) add required reviewers, wait timers, branch restrictions and environment-specific secrets. A job with <code>environment: production</code> pauses until someone approves it, which gives you continuous delivery with a human gate.</p>
+
+<h2>Reusable workflows</h2>
+<p>When several repos share the same pipeline, define it once:</p>
+<pre><code># .github/workflows/build.yml in a shared repo
+on:
+  workflow_call:
+    inputs:
+      image: { type: string, required: true }
+
+# caller repo
+jobs:
+  build:
+    uses: your-org/ci-templates/.github/workflows/build.yml@v1
+    with:
+      image: ghcr.io/your-org/api</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Next steps</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Build the complete pipeline in <a href="projects.html#cicd-pipeline">Project 03</a>.</li>
+  <li>Add security gates: <a href="blog-devsecops-pipeline.html">Security Scanning in CI/CD</a>.</li>
+  <li>Hand deployment to Git: <a href="blog-gitops-argocd.html">GitOps with Argo CD</a>.</li>
+</ul>
diff --git a/_blog/posts/devsecops-pipeline.html b/_blog/posts/devsecops-pipeline.html
index 6e994e2..e4e6f0a 100644
--- a/_blog/posts/devsecops-pipeline.html
+++ b/_blog/posts/devsecops-pipeline.html
@@ -55,3 +55,66 @@ jobs:
 <div class="article-callout">
   <p><strong>Golden rule:</strong> Set <code>exit-code: 1</code> on CRITICAL findings so the pipeline fails and blocks the merge. Security gates only work if they're enforced.</p>
 </div>
+
+<h2>Scan your infrastructure code too</h2>
+<p>Misconfigured Terraform, Kubernetes manifests and Dockerfiles cause more cloud breaches than exotic exploits. Scan them in the same pipeline:</p>
+<pre><code>  iac-scan:
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    steps:
+      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
+      - name: Trivy config scan
+        uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@0.28.0
+        with:
+          scan-type: config
+          scan-ref: .
+          severity: HIGH,CRITICAL
+          exit-code: 1</code></pre>
+<p>Typical findings: security groups open to <code>0.0.0.0/0</code>, unencrypted S3 buckets or EBS volumes, containers running as root, and missing resource limits.</p>
+
+<h2>Generate an SBOM and sign your images</h2>
+<p>A <strong>Software Bill of Materials</strong> lists every component in an image, so when the next big CVE lands you can answer "are we affected?" in seconds. <strong>Signing</strong> proves an image came from your pipeline and wasn't tampered with.</p>
+<pre><code>  sign:
+    needs: container-scan
+    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
+    permissions:
+      id-token: write          # keyless signing via OIDC
+      packages: write
+    steps:
+      - uses: sigstore/cosign-installer@v3
+      - uses: anchore/sbom-action@v0
+        with:
+          image: ghcr.io/you/app:${{ github.sha }}
+          format: spdx-json
+          output-file: sbom.spdx.json
+      - run: |
+          cosign sign --yes ghcr.io/you/app:${{ github.sha }}
+          cosign attest --yes --predicate sbom.spdx.json --type spdxjson ghcr.io/you/app:${{ github.sha }}</code></pre>
+<p>In the cluster, a policy engine such as <strong>Kyverno</strong> can reject any image that isn't signed by your pipeline.</p>
+
+<h2>Keep dependencies patched automatically</h2>
+<pre><code># .github/dependabot.yml
+version: 2
+updates:
+  - package-ecosystem: npm
+    directory: /
+    schedule: { interval: weekly }
+  - package-ecosystem: docker
+    directory: /
+    schedule: { interval: weekly }
+  - package-ecosystem: github-actions
+    directory: /
+    schedule: { interval: weekly }</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Make it stick</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li><strong>Start in report-only mode</strong>, fix the backlog, then switch the gates to blocking. Teams disable noisy gates that block everything on day one.</li>
+  <li><strong>Pin third-party actions</strong> to a version or a commit SHA, not <code>@master</code>.</li>
+  <li><strong>Give the workflow least-privilege <code>permissions:</code></strong> and use OIDC instead of long-lived cloud keys.</li>
+  <li><strong>Turn on GitHub secret scanning and push protection</strong> so secrets are blocked before they land.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2>Next steps</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Add these gates to <a href="projects.html#cicd-pipeline">Project 03</a>, then enforce signatures in the <a href="projects.html#platform-capstone">capstone</a>.</li>
+  <li>Review the <a href="interview.html#security">DevSecOps interview questions</a>.</li>
+</ul>
diff --git a/_blog/posts/k8s-beginners.html b/_blog/posts/k8s-beginners.html
index a24d81e..c5df561 100644
--- a/_blog/posts/k8s-beginners.html
+++ b/_blog/posts/k8s-beginners.html
@@ -86,3 +86,91 @@ kubectl rollout undo deployment/my-app</code></pre>
 <div class="article-callout">
   <p><strong>Practice tip:</strong> Use <code>kind</code> or <code>minikube</code> to run a local Kubernetes cluster on your laptop for free. No cloud account needed.</p>
 </div>
+
+<h2>Run it locally with kind</h2>
+<pre><code># install kind (https://kind.sigs.k8s.io) and kubectl, then:
+kind create cluster --name lab
+kubectl cluster-info --context kind-lab
+kubectl get nodes
+
+# load a locally built image into the cluster (no registry needed)
+docker build -t myapp:1.0 .
+kind load docker-image myapp:1.0 --name lab</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Namespaces</h2>
+<p>Namespaces split one cluster into virtual sub-clusters, such as per team or per environment. Most resources (Pods, Deployments, Services, Secrets) are namespaced; nodes and PersistentVolumes are not.</p>
+<pre><code>kubectl create namespace dev
+kubectl -n dev get all
+kubectl config set-context --current --namespace=dev   # change your default</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Configuration: ConfigMaps and Secrets</h2>
+<p>Keep configuration out of the image so the same image runs in every environment.</p>
+<pre><code>apiVersion: v1
+kind: ConfigMap
+metadata:
+  name: my-app-config
+data:
+  LOG_LEVEL: info
+  FEATURE_X: "true"
+---
+apiVersion: v1
+kind: Secret
+metadata:
+  name: my-app-secret
+type: Opaque
+stringData:
+  DATABASE_URL: postgres://app:change-me@db:5432/app</code></pre>
+<p>Use them in the Deployment's container spec:</p>
+<pre><code>        envFrom:
+        - configMapRef:
+            name: my-app-config
+        - secretRef:
+            name: my-app-secret</code></pre>
+<div class="article-callout warn">
+  <p><strong>Secrets are only base64-encoded, not encrypted.</strong> Don't commit them to Git. Use External Secrets, Sealed Secrets or SOPS (see <a href="blog-gitops-argocd.html">GitOps with Argo CD</a>).</p>
+</div>
+
+<h2>Health checks: readiness and liveness probes</h2>
+<pre><code>        readinessProbe:          # gate traffic until the app is ready
+          httpGet: { path: /ready, port: 8080 }
+          periodSeconds: 5
+        livenessProbe:           # restart the container if it hangs
+          httpGet: { path: /healthz, port: 8080 }
+          initialDelaySeconds: 10
+          periodSeconds: 10</code></pre>
+<p>Without a readiness probe, a rolling update sends traffic to pods before they can serve it. Keep the liveness probe simple: it should check the process itself, not the database.</p>
+
+<h2>Exposing HTTP with an Ingress</h2>
+<p>A Service of type <code>LoadBalancer</code> per app gets expensive. An <strong>Ingress</strong> routes many hostnames and paths through one load balancer, via an Ingress controller such as ingress-nginx or Traefik. The newer <strong>Gateway API</strong> is its successor and works the same way.</p>
+<pre><code>apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
+kind: Ingress
+metadata:
+  name: my-app
+spec:
+  ingressClassName: nginx
+  rules:
+  - host: app.example.com
+    http:
+      paths:
+      - path: /
+        pathType: Prefix
+        backend:
+          service:
+            name: my-app-service
+            port:
+              number: 80</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Debugging workflow</h2>
+<pre><code>kubectl get pods                          # STATUS and RESTARTS
+kubectl describe pod &lt;pod&gt;                # the Events section explains most failures
+kubectl logs &lt;pod&gt; --previous             # logs from the last crash
+kubectl get events --sort-by=.lastTimestamp
+kubectl port-forward svc/my-app-service 8080:80   # test a Service from your laptop</code></pre>
+<p>For specific errors, see the <a href="troubleshooting.html#kubernetes">Kubernetes troubleshooting playbooks</a>.</p>
+
+<h2>Next steps</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Package your manifests as a Helm chart: <a href="projects.html#k8s-helm">Project 05</a>.</li>
+  <li>Deploy from Git automatically: <a href="blog-gitops-argocd.html">GitOps with Argo CD</a>.</li>
+  <li>Keep the <a href="cheatsheets.html#kubernetes">kubectl cheatsheet</a> handy.</li>
+</ul>
diff --git a/_blog/posts/prometheus-grafana.html b/_blog/posts/prometheus-grafana.html
index 18ebd80..70ab9ce 100644
--- a/_blog/posts/prometheus-grafana.html
+++ b/_blog/posts/prometheus-grafana.html
@@ -43,3 +43,64 @@ histogram_quantile(0.99,
 <div class="article-callout">
   <p><strong>Import dashboards:</strong> Grafana has thousands of pre-built dashboards. Try ID <code>1860</code> for Node Exporter Full — an excellent starting point for server monitoring.</p>
 </div>
+
+<h2>How Prometheus works</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Prometheus <strong>pulls</strong> (scrapes) metrics over HTTP from <code>/metrics</code> endpoints at a regular interval.</li>
+  <li>Metrics are time series identified by a name and <strong>labels</strong>, e.g. <code>http_requests_total{method="GET",code="200"}</code>.</li>
+  <li>Four metric types: <strong>counter</strong> (only goes up), <strong>gauge</strong> (goes up and down), <strong>histogram</strong> (bucketed observations, for latency) and <strong>summary</strong>.</li>
+  <li>With kube-prometheus-stack, you tell Prometheus what to scrape with <strong>ServiceMonitor</strong> resources.</li>
+</ul>
+
+<h2>Scrape your own app</h2>
+<pre><code>apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
+kind: ServiceMonitor
+metadata:
+  name: my-app
+  namespace: monitoring
+  labels:
+    release: monitoring        # must match the stack's selector
+spec:
+  namespaceSelector:
+    matchNames: [default]
+  selector:
+    matchLabels:
+      app: my-app
+  endpoints:
+  - port: http                 # named port on the Service
+    path: /metrics
+    interval: 30s</code></pre>
+<p>Check <strong>Status → Targets</strong> in the Prometheus UI (<code>kubectl -n monitoring port-forward svc/monitoring-kube-prometheus-prometheus 9090</code>) to confirm the target is <code>UP</code>.</p>
+
+<h2>Alerting rules</h2>
+<pre><code>apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1
+kind: PrometheusRule
+metadata:
+  name: my-app-alerts
+  namespace: monitoring
+  labels:
+    release: monitoring
+spec:
+  groups:
+  - name: my-app
+    rules:
+    - alert: HighErrorRate
+      expr: |
+        sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="my-app",status=~"5.."}[5m]))
+          / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="my-app"}[5m])) &gt; 0.05
+      for: 10m
+      labels:
+        severity: page
+      annotations:
+        summary: "my-app 5xx rate above 5% for 10 minutes"
+        runbook_url: https://example.com/runbooks/my-app-errors</code></pre>
+<p>Alertmanager then routes alerts to Slack, PagerDuty or email. For better alerts based on user-facing SLOs, see <a href="blog-sre-fundamentals.html">SRE Fundamentals</a>.</p>
+
+<h2>Logs and traces</h2>
+<p>Metrics tell you <em>that</em> something is wrong. Logs and traces tell you <em>why</em>. Add <strong>Loki</strong> (logs) and <strong>Tempo</strong> or <strong>Jaeger</strong> (traces) as Grafana data sources, and instrument apps with <strong>OpenTelemetry</strong> so you can jump from a latency spike to the exact slow request.</p>
+
+<h2>Next steps</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Build a full metrics, logs and SLO-alerting stack in <a href="projects.html#observability">Project 06</a>.</li>
+  <li>Keep the <a href="cheatsheets.html#prometheus">PromQL cheatsheet</a> handy.</li>
+</ul>
diff --git a/_blog/posts/terraform-aws.html b/_blog/posts/terraform-aws.html
index d5f9f25..e1ab698 100644
--- a/_blog/posts/terraform-aws.html
+++ b/_blog/posts/terraform-aws.html
@@ -78,3 +78,77 @@ terraform destroy</code></pre>
 <div class="article-callout">
   <p><strong>Always review</strong> <code>terraform plan</code> output before applying. It shows exactly what will be created, modified, or destroyed.</p>
 </div>
+
+<h2>Variables and outputs</h2>
+<pre><code># variables.tf
+variable "region" {
+  type    = string
+  default = "eu-west-1"
+}
+
+variable "instance_type" {
+  type        = string
+  default     = "t3.micro"
+  description = "EC2 size for the web server"
+}
+
+# outputs.tf
+output "instance_id" {
+  value = aws_instance.web.id
+}
+
+# terraform.tfvars
+region = "eu-west-1"</code></pre>
+
+<h2>Remote state with locking</h2>
+<p>By default, state lives in a local <code>terraform.tfstate</code> file. That breaks as soon as two people or a CI job are involved. Store it in S3 instead; Terraform 1.10+ can lock it natively, with no DynamoDB table needed:</p>
+<pre><code>terraform {
+  backend "s3" {
+    bucket       = "yourname-terraform-state"   # create once, with versioning on
+    key          = "dev/network/terraform.tfstate"
+    region       = "eu-west-1"
+    encrypt      = true
+    use_lockfile = true
+  }
+}</code></pre>
+<p>After adding a backend, run <code>terraform init -migrate-state</code> to move the existing state.</p>
+
+<h2>Modules: write once, reuse everywhere</h2>
+<pre><code>modules/
+└── web/
+    ├── main.tf       # resources
+    ├── variables.tf  # inputs
+    └── outputs.tf    # outputs
+
+# envs/dev/main.tf
+module "web" {
+  source        = "../../modules/web"
+  instance_type = "t3.micro"
+}
+
+# envs/prod/main.tf
+module "web" {
+  source        = "../../modules/web"
+  instance_type = "m7g.large"
+}</code></pre>
+<p>For common building blocks, the community modules (e.g. <code>terraform-aws-modules/vpc/aws</code>) are well tested. Pin their versions.</p>
+
+<h2>Terraform in CI</h2>
+<pre><code>terraform fmt -check -recursive
+terraform init -input=false
+terraform validate
+terraform plan -input=false -out=tf.plan     # post this output on the PR
+# after review and merge:
+terraform apply -input=false tf.plan</code></pre>
+<p>Add <code>tflint</code> and a misconfiguration scanner (<code>trivy config</code> or <code>checkov</code>) to catch open security groups and unencrypted storage before they reach AWS.</p>
+
+<div class="article-callout warn">
+  <p><strong>Clean up:</strong> run <code>terraform destroy</code> when you finish experimenting, and set an AWS Budget alert. Forgotten NAT gateways and load balancers are the most common surprise bills.</p>
+</div>
+
+<h2>Next steps</h2>
+<ul>
+  <li>Build a multi-AZ network and web tier in <a href="projects.html#terraform-aws">Project 04</a>.</li>
+  <li>Learn the services you're provisioning: <a href="blog-aws-core-services.html">AWS Core Services</a>.</li>
+  <li>When things go wrong: <a href="troubleshooting.html#terraform">Terraform troubleshooting playbooks</a>.</li>
+</ul>
